  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nPockets of fog, locally dense, have been observed in several \nlocations as xover temps are exceeded. These pockets appear to \nbe somewhat shallow in nature and should begin to lessen some as\ncombination of high clouds and a slight increase in boundary \nlayer winds occurs. Not planning any headlines, but will monitor\ntrends if highlighted factors don't impact the coverage. \n\nAny fog will quickly burn off as plenty of sunshine and warming \ntemperatures end the threat. Low pressure and a frontal boundary \nwill remain well north of the area, with an increasing SW flow \nresulting in strong WAA. This will set the stage for well above \nnormal (possible near record) high temperatures into the 80s. Model \nblends hint at a few 90 degrees highs on Tuesday. Any precip chances \nappear to remain in MI. \n\nThe frontal boundary will slowly push south into the area Wed and \nThur as a series of waves move along it with chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. Some uncertainty exists exactly how much progress the \nfront makes each day given the lack of upper level energy to help \npush it along. Periods of likely to cat pops remain. \n\nThursday night into Friday, models continue to vary on interactions \n(or lack thereof) with northern and southern stream waves. That \nbeing said, there may be some agreement starting as an initial, \nweaker southern stream wave tries to phase with the northern stream \ntrough, with an orphaned upper low sticking around in the SW US.  As \na result, picture remains unclear as to exact potential impacts for \nthis period and into the weekend. In the wake of the system, at \nleast a brief shot of colder (below normal) air arrives.",
